.TH AMUX 4
.SH NAME
amux \- sessions on acme's windows, each with its own address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B amux
[
.B -Di
] [
.B -a
.I acme
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Amux
lets more than one program edit one
.IR acme (1)
window at once.
A window's
.BR addr ,
its limit and the guard written to its
.B seq
belong to the window, so each program that writes them moves the others'.
.I Amux
gives each program a session on the window, with its own address, limit and guard,
and makes each session's use of them on acme one step that no other session comes between.
It posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
.RB ( /srv/amux
by default) and mounts itself on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/amux ).
It mounts
.BR $acmesrv ,
the
.B /srv
file of an acme
(which
.B acmestart
sets),
on
.I acme
.RB ( /mnt/acme )
in a namespace of its own, when a program first looks.
The
.B -D
option prints the 9P messages;
.B -i
serves 9P on standard input and output, and posts and mounts nothing.
.PP
The top directory has a directory for each of acme's windows, named by the window's ID.
Each holds:
.TF clone
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a session, and reading it gives the session's number
.IR n .
Writing it is writing the session's
.BR ctl .
The session lasts while any file of it is open or walked to.
.TP
.I n
The directory of session
.IR n ,
which holds the files of acme's window directory, in
.IR acme (4)'s
formats.
.PD
.PP
A program that owns a window can bind the session over it,
.IP
.EX
bind /mnt/amux/$winid/$n /mnt/acme/$winid
.EE
.PP
and run as it would on acme.
In a session:
.TF limit=addr
.TP
.B addr
is the session's, as acme's is the window's:
the first open of it in the session sets it to
.BR #0,#0 ,
and reading it clamps it to the body and gives its value.
A write of a character address,
.BI # m
or
.BI # m ,# n\fR,
sets it after acme checks it is in the body;
any other address acme evaluates from the session's address and limit, and the session
takes the result.
.TP
.B data
reads and writes at the session's address, which moves as acme's would:
after a write of
.I n
runes it is
.BI # q0+n ,# q0+n\fR,
and after a read both ends are past the runes read.
.TP
.B xdata
reads at the session's address up to its end, and moves its start past the runes read.
.TP
.B seq
Writing a number guards the session's next open of
.BR body ,
.BR data ,
.BR xdata ,
.B style
or
.BR rdevent ,
and nothing another session opens.
Reading it is reading acme's.
.TP
.B style
After a write of the session's
.BR addr ,
a write of
.B style
replaces the styles at the session's address, as acme's does after a write of acme's;
otherwise it replaces the window's styles, whatever another session's address.
.TP
.B event
is acme's, and belongs to one session at a time:
the first to open it holds it and may open it again,
and another session's open fails,
.LR "event in use by another session" ,
until every open of the holder's has closed.
.TP
.B ctl
finds its messages as acme does, and handles
.BR addr=dot ,
.B dot=addr
and
.B limit=addr
with the session's address and limit, refuses
.B lock
and
.BR unlock ,
and passes every other message to acme's.
.PD
.PP
The other files pass to acme's.
Reads of
.B event
and
.BR rdevent ,
which wait, each wait in a process of their own,
so a program that never reads holds up no other.
.SH EXAMPLES
Two sessions on window 5: an address the second writes does not move the first's.
.IP
.EX
{
	{
		echo -n 2 >/mnt/amux/5/1/addr
		echo -n 3 >/mnt/amux/5/2/addr
		echo -n new >/mnt/amux/5/2/data
		echo -n old >/mnt/amux/5/1/data
	} <>[4]/mnt/amux/5/clone
} <>[3]/mnt/amux/5/clone
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/amux.c
.br
.B rc/bin/acmestart
.br
its design, plan9web's
.BR doc/amux.md ,
and its model,
.B specs/amux.qnt
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR acme (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR astyles (4)
.SH BUGS
A program that writes acme's
.B addr
or opens its guarded files directly, around
.IR amux ,
can still come between a session's steps, as any program can on acme.
.br
A program that opens acme's
.B event
directly, around
.IR amux ,
shares its one stream with the session that holds it.
.br
.I Amux
does every session's work on acme in one loop, so a ctl message acme is slow over, such as
.BR put ,
holds up every session until acme answers.
.br
It does not exit when its acme does.
